    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for my circuits final and i'm a bit stuck. can u explain how a capacitor acts differently when it's in a DC circuit vs an AC circuit? also whats the formula for capacitive reactance? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor initially acts as a high resistance, blocking current flow until it charges up to the applied voltage. Once fully charged, it behaves like an open circuit and no current flows. In an AC circuit, the capacitor continuously charges and discharges as the polarity of the AC signal changes, allowing current to flow. The formula for capacitive reactance \\(X_C\\) is \\(X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi f C}\\), where \\(f\\) is the frequency of the AC signal and \\(C\\) is the capacitance.",
